let's test the collector logic against the capture file first to make sure the frame parsing/merging/decoding path works, then we'll test against live dongle. I need to make a small file-based test that doesn't actually write to InfluxDB.","I broke the file order with that edit. The `config_from_env` function is defined before `CollectorConfig`. Let me read and fix:","Now let's run the file-based test again:","Now let's test against the live dongle. I'll run the test_collector.py for ~20 seconds to verify it connects, parses, and prints snapshots:","Great — the live connection works! InfluxDB isn't running locally, so writes fail as expected. But frames are coming in and registers are merging to 381. Let me kill this process and update the test script to disable InfluxDB writes for pure testing:","Good — collector connects and parses live data. Now I need to:\n\n1. Improve test_collector.py to skip InfluxDB and just print snapshots\n2. Make the collector handle InfluxDB connection failures gracefully (warn once, retry)\n3. Set up InfluxDB locally so we can actually write and query data\n4. Commit Phase 2\n\nLet me fix the test script first so it doesn't depend on InfluxDB:","Now let's also make the collector more graceful about InfluxDB being unavailable — log a warning, not a full traceback every cycle. But first, let me test the updated test script live for 15 seconds:","The dongle is closing the connection immediately every time. This is likely because the dongle expects the SolarAssistant client to maintain a specific connection behavior, or only allows one connection at a time. Earlier when we captured 30 seconds successfully, we used a script that read once and kept the socket open. Let me kill this and investigate the dongle behavior:","Interesting — the dongle is now closing the connection immediately, even though earlier we got 30 seconds of data. This suggests the dongle only accepts one TCP connection and something else may be connected to it, or it's in a different state. The SolarAssistant software might be running on another device and holding the connection.\n\nLet me check what other hosts might be connected, or if there's an active SolarAssistant Pi on the network:","The dongle closes connection immediately now, but earlier it gave 30s of data. This is a key characteristic of the LuxPower dongle: **it only accepts ONE concurrent TCP connection**, and something else may be connected now (probably the SolarAssistant Pi or another client). We need to either:\n\n1. Disconnect the existing SolarAssistant setup from the dongle\n2. Run our collector on a device that has exclusive access\n3. Use the Pi running SolarAssistant if it's accessible, or replace it\n\nLet me check if there's a SolarAssistant process or device on the network. Also we should try nmap/ping for common SolarAssistant Pi addresses and SSH into the alpha Pi to see if it's running there:","Not on alpha. Let me check if any other host on the network is holding port 8000 open. I'll scan the common SolarAssistant range:","Those are alpha's multiple IPs and your Mac (.5). No SolarAssistant Pi visible on the network. The dongle is just being picky. \n\nLooking at the earlier successful capture, the dongle gave data for ~30 seconds before closing. The current behavior (immediate close) suggests either:\n1. The dongle has a connection cooldown / rate limit\n2. It detected a non-LuxPower client and blacklisted us temporarily\n3. It needs some initial handshake/register message\n\nLet me check the lxp-bridge code to see if there's an expected initial handshake, or if we need to register as a specific client type. The dongle may expect a login message first:","No special handshake — lxp-bridge just connects and reads. The dongle auto-broadcasts. So why did it close immediately this time but not earlier?\n\nPossibly because the dongle has a **single-connection limit** and we or something else held it open recently. Or maybe it's still \"owned\" by our previous connection. Let me wait a bit and retry, and also use `SO_KEEPALIVE`:","The dongle is consistently closing immediately now. This is very different from earlier. Let me check if maybe the dongle only talks to one specific MAC/IP and is locked to the SolarAssistant host. Or maybe we need to send a heartbeat/login packet back.\n\nLooking at the lxp-bridge code more carefully — when the dongle sends a Heartbeat (0xC1), the coordinator sends a reply. Let me check the heartbeat handling:","Hmm, lxp-bridge does NOT reply to heartbeats. So that's not the issue. Let me look at the protocol spec to see if there's an initial message expected:","This is the doc I wrote earlier (it's our own docs/reference/lux-protocol/PROTOCOL.md), and it confirms: the dongle sends unsolicited data every ~2 seconds without any handshake. So the immediate close now is odd.\n\nWait — earlier I generated a fresh SSH key `~/.ssh/id_ed25519` for GitHub, but maybe I accidentally replaced the previous key that was being used? No, unrelated.\n\nThe most likely explanation: the dongle only allows **one concurrent TCP connection**, and our earlier 30-second capture session held the connection. Then when the next test ran, the dongle was still in some state or our previous socket was lingering. But now even waiting several minutes, it still closes immediately.\n\nLet me check the dongle behavior with a longer timeout and see if it's actually sending data that we're missing due to a parsing issue:","The dongle is definitely refusing to send data right now. This is a real hardware/network state issue, not a parser issue. Possibilities:\n\n1. **Single-connection lock**: Some other device has the connection. But our scan didn't find a SolarAssistant Pi.\n2. **The dongle requires a specific source IP/MAC**: Jeremy's Mac got blacklisted or the dongle is paired to another host.\n3. **Dongle is in a fault/cooldown state**: Maybe after too many connects it needs time.\n4. **The previous connection from the earlier 30s capture is still \"owned\"**: Some TCP state issue.\n\nSince we already have a validated parser and a working collector design, and we can't get fresh live data right now, I should:\n\n1. Build a **replay collector mode** that reads from the capture file for testing\n2. Commit what we have as Phase 2 milestone\n3. Document the single-connection behavior\n4. Test live again later\n\nActually, let me try one more thing: the dongle might expect us to send a packet first. The lxp-bridge doc says \"Without sending any requests, the dongle pushes...\" but maybe the specific EG4 6000XP firmware differs. Let me try sending a ReadInput request for register 0 and see if it responds:","The dongle is not responding to acti,"95":"0x0003","96":"0x0001","97":"0x0118","98":"0xFFF3","99":"0x0000"},"register_type":"input","serial":"4372830102","utc_timestamp":1742532722}
